1063 #edb/ref 17592186107162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106644 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106644 #edb/ref 1061 187129 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106644 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2d241585-6a66-55ed-8dd4-a690566db2a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106644 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106645 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106645 #edb/ref 1061 187132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106645 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80b138e7-13bc-505c-9c7b-e57531dd484d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106645 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106646 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106646 #edb/ref 1061 187135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106646 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c27f1faa-2fca-5dc4-8283-8cf462884593" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106646 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106647 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186106648 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106647 #edb/ref 1061 187138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106647 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f65fca30-a9ac-5101-b87a-1c01743c0b0b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106647 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186105752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106648 #edb/ref 1061 187141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106648 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ee786271-6101-5bf4-9d57-2fe763770d7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186106648 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186107163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107162 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider the polygon $ABCD$, where the side $\\overline{AB}$ is extended beyond the vertex $B.$\n\n![](images/fa/fa23c036920e2c506c38f1e7be8a498c0a349ef145e8c444201665345bc648e4.png)\n\nWe call $\\color{blue}\\angle{CBE}$ the **exterior angle** of the vertex $B$.\n\nGiven a polygon's vertex, the interior and exterior angles are supplementary. So, for the vertex $B,$ we have:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{\\color{YellowOrange}m\\angle{ABC}}_{\\large\\text{Interior}} \\:+\\: \\underbrace{\\color{blue}m\\angle{CBE}}_{\\large\\text{Exterior}} = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nWe have the following theorem:\n\nThe measures of the exterior angles of any simple polygon sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\nWe'll show why this theorem is true at the end of the lesson.\n\nLet's label the exterior angles of our polygon below:\n\n![](images/ac/acc0ac90f27a78e83b6b14651f61ddfe6a87afa56b6e51a1af57f5e74c31689f.png)\n\nAccording to our theorem, these four angles sum to $360^\\circ.$\n\n$$\na+b+c+d = 360^\\circ\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107162 #edb/ref 1065 8041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107162 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6cb4a5ba-6c28-59e2-ab72-89e4f5f69ae3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107162 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107163 #edb/ref 1064 "Why do the exterior angles of a polygon always sum to $360^\\circ?$\n\nSuppose we have an $n$-sided polygon. Then, we have the following:\n\n- There are $n$ pairs of interior-exterior angles, each pair being supplementary (i.e., their measures add to $180^\\circ).$\n\n- Therefore, the sum of the measures of *all* interior-exterior pairs equals ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}.$\n\n- In a previous lesson, we saw that the sum of the *interior* angles equals ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}.$\n\nTherefore, to get the sum of the *exterior* angles, we subtract ${\\color{red}{(n-2)\\cdot 180^\\circ}}$ from ${\\color{blue}n\\cdot 180^\\circ}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Sum of Exterior Angles} & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-(n-2)\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘}-n\\cdot {180}^{∘}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}-\\cancel{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}+2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis proves that the sum of the exterior angles of a (simple) polygon equals $360^\\circ.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107163 #edb/ref 1065 8042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107163 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fb5a1532-47ec-5455-96c8-49187460e543" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186107163 #edb/ref 1067 "Proving the Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050208 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186110915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111165 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567016 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1039 "Combining the Rules of Exponents Without Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110661 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "854edbd5-3aef-5c6b-bb79-967aa241beb2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111192 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1039 "Combining the Rules of Exponents With Fractions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110300 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110662 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "736c1b75-fe60-5f0a-b426-a19f3edaaa2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110663 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049089 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111333 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186111391 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110663 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186499524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110663 #edb/ref 1039 "Combining the Rules of Exponents With Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110663 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186110301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110663 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "014ea973-359e-53f5-9151-adc85e08bd3f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186111601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1045 "Combining the Rules of Exponents With Algebraic Expressions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049252 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110813 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "7fe1fb12-e10b-5870-8007-02f1eb2f68f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111594 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111595 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111594 #edb/ref 1061 3561 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111594 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ff971efe-57b1-5d6a-a9d6-53728830ae56" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111594 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111824 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111595 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111595 #edb/ref 1061 3562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111595 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a787c3de-89d7-50ac-b1ff-50a2fb31a2ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111595 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111596 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111596 #edb/ref 1061 3565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111596 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8884d25c-0665-5e4b-b13b-866ef3ec66f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111596 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111597 #edb/ref 1061 3569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111597 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "73248b69-f18a-503e-b712-b3f56ae6841a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111597 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111601 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111601 #edb/ref 1061 37772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111601 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8d59c7e-2b45-5493-a09c-f4211a690c16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111601 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111904 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111824 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we might need to use more than one rule of exponents to simplify an expression. First, let's remind ourselves of all of the rules of exponents.\n\n- Radicals as fractional exponents: $\\sqrt[n]{x} = x^{1/n}$\n\n- The product rule: $a^n \\cdot a^m = a^{n+m}$\n\n- The quotient rule: $\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m} = a^{n-m}$\n\n- The power rule: $\\left( a^m \\right)^n = a^{mn}$\n\n- The power of product rule: $(ab)^n = a^nb^n$\n\n- The power of quotient rule: $\\left(\\dfrac a b \\right)^n = \\dfrac{a^n}{b^n}$\n\nAs an example, let's simplify the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\left(u^2 v\\right)^3\n$$\n\nWe start by using the power of product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}^{2}v)}^{3}={({u}^{2})}^{3}\\cdot {v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({u}^{2})}^{3}\\cdot {v}^{3} & = \\\\ {u}^{(2\\cdot 3)}\\cdot {v}^{3} & = \\\\ {u}^{6}\\cdot {v}^{3} & = \\\\ {u}^{6}{v}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111824 #edb/ref 1065 1485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111824 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "13b1c64a-c868-5824-919e-c3a665bb4a96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111824 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111904 #edb/ref 1064 "To simplify the expression $x\\cdot \\sqrt[3]{x^2},$ we first rewrite the radical as a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\nx \\cdot \\sqrt[3]{x^2} = x \\cdot \\left( x^2 \\right)^{1/3}\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the power rule for exponents:\n\n$$\nx \\cdot \\left( x^2 \\right)^{1/3} = x \\cdot x^{2(1/3)} = x \\cdot x^{2/3}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {x}^{2/3} & = \\\\ {x}^{1+2/3} & = \\\\ {x}^{5/3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we express the result as a radical:\n\n$$\nx^{5/3} = \\sqrt[3]{x^5}\n$$\n\nNotice that, by combining the radicals as fractional exponents and power rules, we get the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt[n]{x^m} = x^{m/n}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111904 #edb/ref 1065 6221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111904 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "91debf57-af23-518b-b432-ea04beccc17a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111904 #edb/ref 1067 "Rules of Exponents and Radicals" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566372 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566414 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566543 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112872 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186566632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112872 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112872 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112872 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186566632 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186112872 #edb/ref 