70 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116271 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116270 #edb/ref 1061 3656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116270 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "878c51c5-4761-5147-9a81-447753be3f73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116270 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115337 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116271 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116271 #edb/ref 1061 3660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116271 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16a2910f-29b2-5b2e-9ad5-d70273d4a38d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116271 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116430 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116272 #edb/ref 1061 3661 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116272 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7450e4ef-df70-5ca4-8c6d-12844e5fe1ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116272 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115338 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116396 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116396 #edb/ref 1061 9151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116396 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4231f520-72fe-5c14-8733-25cc76a6e65e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116396 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116449 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116397 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116397 #edb/ref 1061 9154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116397 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52e4c58c-e58e-5223-98e5-cc31383de47c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116397 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116398 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116398 #edb/ref 1061 9158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116398 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9f034d0-1d0e-54ea-83b8-ad683048d5c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116398 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116268 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1061 9160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25c91feb-798e-5857-9090-3b36088bc71b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116399 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116429 #edb/ref 1064 "A function ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) is **strictly increasing** if its ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-value always rises as we increase the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-value. No section of the graph is allowed to be flat.\n\n![](images/f6/f6136545f441473a1c873f7d3296e8d96c23357825ac442ce83ce313f2d74199.png)\n\nOn the other hand, a function ![](images/93/93f888a1ee0b7da5e29f442f803f1c0eaf73ad02f5eaa97e85bb8a35d607015b.svg) is **increasing** if its ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-value *never falls* as we increase the ![](images/fc/fcc5df999e3b49fae1685ee0ab5bd03709804e1dd49f2ee722c4269eb0f5506d.svg)-value. \n\n**Watch Out!** Increasing functions, like the one below, are allowed to have flat sections.\n\n![](images/b9/b979741f27906322c7ff290ae37bb5214c0c88d30e504fe18569c1f957e3d72e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116429 #edb/ref 1065 1500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116429 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "75e3de1d-9fd6-536a-b21e-7d6434297ce7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116429 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116430 #edb/ref 1064 "A function $f(x)$ is **strictly decreasing** if its $y$-value always *falls* as we increase the $x$-value. No section of the graph is allowed to be flat.\n\n![](images/b8/b8a2d54cedc84f159a61bfccc11bdab9adc73eb54b694dd1167802998f4d39d3.png)\n\nOn the other hand, a function $f(x)$ is **decreasing** if its $y$-value *never rises* as we increase the $x$-value. Decreasing functions are allowed to have flat sections.\n\n![](images/4f/4f91b34a57ac42ba82e937c78c74bcb26677e1d6fbf8ed40865f6db73b1cf613.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116430 #edb/ref 1065 1501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116430 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "81ea8002-60eb-5227-bbdd-bc329f9f3f89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116430 #edb/ref 1067 "Decreasing and Strictly Decreasing Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1064 "Consider $\\triangle ABC$ shown below. \n\n![](images/6f/6fdb9a8337d16f1ef1910989b3df92844c5393c33108f98ae7993cc939bed0b3.png)\n\nNow, let's draw a line segment that \n\n- runs from $\\color{blue}C$ to the line ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}},$ and\n\n- is *perpendicular* to ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}.$\n\nThis line segment is the dashed segment shown below:\n\n![](images/4a/4a7ee2771c9b0c60dbb07c43b0439e822ea9b2c261ac41af9b44fec72b9f2a91.png)\n\nFinally, let's label the other endpoint of our segment ${\\color{blue}{H}}.$\n\n![](images/75/758eab533719220515460b6677054438a9ac0e2701d1a5267b973e526f63bd90.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The line segment ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}$ is called the **height** (or **altitude**) *corresponding to* ${\\color{red}\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}}.$\n\n- The line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{AB}$ is called the **base** of the altitude ${\\color{blue}\\overline{CH}}.$\n\nAs we'll soon discover, the heights and their corresponding bases are important attributes of triangles." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1065 2232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bdb9bfbd-b830-51f0-8712-5d34f83f2812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116449 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1064 "We can draw exactly three heights (or altitudes) in any triangle. However, sometimes the height lies outside the triangle.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider $\\triangle{ABC}$ below.\n\n![](images/df/df1ac2c1074305f8ead506d9aeb8c3927ad51ae5949b45976f1971b52d402ee5.png)\n\nThe height corresponding to $\\overline{BC}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $A$ to the line $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$\n\nIn this case, it's impossible to draw a perpendicular from $A$ to the *segment* $\\overline{BC}.$ However, we can first *extend* the line segment $\\overline{BC}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/26/26bf9eb8af1e9b71c263c08cae5797d5c911410985cdc01213ac2c295f7b8d46.png)\n\nNow, we draw the perpendicular from $A$ to $\\overset{\\longleftrightarrow}{BC}.$ This gives the height $\\overline{AH}{:}$\n\n![](images/89/894686fce1393ebcf998be6885020627c9f3cde5916adfec67234f15f8895826.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1065 2236 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "4031d801-f4b8-543f-ad4d-9ef60d513427" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116450 #edb/ref 1067 "Case of Extended Bases" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567022 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1039 "Factoring a Trinomial With a Negative Quadratic Term" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186175797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186175949 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "2d960485-d99c-555f-abb6-8e4d0564913e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176014 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176014 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186176539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176014 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176014 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186500331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176014 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186559774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176014 #edb/ref 