ation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176168 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186176837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176168 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176168 #edb/ref 1050 600.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176168 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b9035507-8754-5ae2-a1ef-580b9406be50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176675 #edb/ref 1061 25611 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176675 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a3a92aca-9db7-5c85-bff4-92c8a5b8ea08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176675 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186175949 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176786 #edb/ref 1061 34865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176786 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d5420dd5-0691-5226-8163-3d5f81ffc20f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176786 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176787 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176787 #edb/ref 1061 34868 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176787 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9bec95a6-c4cf-5bb2-9b8d-d3c10163aa97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176787 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176015 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176791 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176791 #edb/ref 1061 34964 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176791 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3b1ff76e-dab3-5822-9f2b-e01a3ba037bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176791 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186177027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176837 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176837 #edb/ref 1061 3737 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176837 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7f194aeb-30f4-5e7e-8022-d36f14b0c127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176837 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176883 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176883 #edb/ref 1061 4262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176883 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1445b874-9aa3-5b17-b1f5-d21b64581d54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176883 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176917 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176917 #edb/ref 1061 4995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176917 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14b0e70f-af6c-594e-b86d-ef230d231ad1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176917 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176918 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186176883 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176918 #edb/ref 1061 5062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176918 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5ed6467d-dfe0-510d-a23b-700569abb8d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176918 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186176965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176961 #edb/ref 1064 "When an object moves at a constant speed $v,$ the distance $d$ that it travels and the time $t$ are related by the equation\n\n$$\nd = vt.\n$$\n\nSuppose that we want to know how long it takes for an object to travel a *fixed* distance. We start by rewriting the above equation for $t\\mathbin :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{d}{v}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $d$ is fixed (i.e. constant), it follows that the time $t$ varies inversely with the velocity $v,$ and $d$ is the constant of variation. \n\nIntuitively, the fact that this is an inverse variation makes sense. The larger the speed of our object, the smaller the time it takes to cover a fixed distance, and vice-versa.\n\nSuppose we're told that an object takes $t=10$ seconds to cover a particular distance when traveling with a speed of $v=5$ meters per second. Then, we can solve for $d$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{d}{v} \\\\ 10 & =\\frac{d}{5} \\\\ 10\\cdot 5 & =d \\\\ 50 & =d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the inverse variation equation\n\n$$\nt = \\dfrac{50}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe can now use this equation to find the amount of time the object takes to cover the same fixed distance for different speeds." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176961 #edb/ref 1065 1512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176961 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d738193b-1de7-58d9-b145-0f84d66d9c3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176961 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176965 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to factor the polynomial \n\n$$\n8x^2 + 24x + 16.\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it looks tricky because the coefficients are rather large! \n\nHowever, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $8.$ If we first factor out an $8$ from each of the terms, then we can cut the factoring down to something more manageable:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t8x^2 + 24x + 16 &= \\[5pt]\n\t\t8(x^2 + 3x + 2)\n\t}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the polynomial $x^2 + 3x + 2$ in the parentheses.\n\nThe constant term $(2)$ has a single factor pair, namely\n\n$$\n(1, 2).\n$$\n\n \n\nNotice that the numbers in this factor pair indeed sum to the linear term's coefficient $(1+2=3).$ \n\nSo, we can write the linear term as $3x=x+2x$ and factor as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x+2 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+x+2x+2 & = \\\\ ({x}^{2}+x)+(2x+2) & = \\\\ x(x+1)+2(x+1) & = \\\\ (x+2)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{2}+24x+16 & = \\\\ 8({x}^{2}+3x+2) & = \\\\ 8(x+2)(x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176965 #edb/ref 1065 1722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176965 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7336a5d6-0b38-5ba4-b278-aaac6a402ece" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186176965 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177027 #edb/ref 1064 "![](images/a2/a2bbedb8a024b6cd75c333e2c0cf025558b71eafeb7215d9c7dbf2fbd678230a.png)\n\nIt is known that the gravitational force $F,$ measured in newtons, that the earth exerts on the moon varies inversely with the **square** of the distance $r,$ in kilometers, between their centers.\n\nAccording to this information, the equation that relates $F$ and $r$ is\n\n$$\nF = \\dfrac{k}{r^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant of variation. This type of relationship is called an **inverse square law**.\n\nAs it turns out, many important physical laws take the form of an inverse square law. Let's see another example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177027 #edb/ref 1065 5696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177027 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6e6cc0ce-3bd6-5947-b049-60a63924bd23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186177027 #edb/ref 1067 "Inverse Square Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050174 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180234 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186332960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516820 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating an Amount Given a Ratio" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179817 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0e44aa33-0aca-5cb2-8c89-6391240beed8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186180235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275171 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186275172 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186357529 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186504652 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516845 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186567084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating an Amount Given a Ratio as a Fraction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186179495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179818 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b5406de3-483e-56c2-b9c6-73de6769552e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186180638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1045 "Further Reasoning With Equivalent Ratios" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186179889 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d591686e-75aa-506f-aade-856d6dea793a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180637 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1061 637169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a4a7f682-c223-50f1-a3eb-6b1f79aad9b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180636 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1061 637170 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "91f76525-9d6e-5ea9-a6a3-b6249f246583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180637 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180638 #edb/ref 1061 637173 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180638 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5f14a9d9-61a5-5432-ad78-2673e2e5e50e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180638 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1064 "Sometimes, we can use ratios to find quantities that aren't explicitly given in the ratio.\n\nFor instance, suppose that the ratio of boys to girls in the fifth grade is $3: 4.$ If there are $15$ boys, how many students are there in total?\n\nNotice that the \"total number of students\" doesn't appear in the ratio. However, we can find the number of students by adding the total number of boys and the number of girls.\n\nWe start with the ratio ${\\color{blue}3}:{\\color{red}4}$ and write it as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}}{{\\color{red}4}}\n$$\n\nIn this fraction, the numerator represents the number of boys and the denominator represents the number of girls.\n\nWe know that there are $15$ boys, so we want to make a numerator of $15.$ We can do this by multiplying the numerator and denominator by $5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{{\\color{blue}3}\\times 5}{{\\color{red}4}\\times 5} = \\dfrac{{\\color{blue}15}}{{\\color{red}20}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since there are $15$ boys in the fifth grade, there must also be $20$ girls. So the total number of students is \n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}15} + {\\color{red}20} = 35\\,.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1065 2051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ca3cabae-8ebe-5879-b608-9d0ebdb233fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180660 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250824 #edb/ref 1080 "images/11/1115307cc21acb99416f137a4e79ef0f2e93ca284db1006d968fed3afebc803c.png" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250824 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250824 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ffa339d-52f0-58f8-a4c8-c